-rf sw4 && git clone -q \"$HT_FORGEJO_URL/world/story-repo.wiki.git\" sw4 && cd sw4 && git log --oneline | head -2 && git diff HEAD~1 | grep '^[+-]' | cut -c1-1200", "shown": {"trace": {"input": [["command", "cd /tmp && rm -rf sw4 && git clone -q \"$HT_FORGEJO_URL/world/story-repo.wiki.git\" sw4 && cd sw4 && git log --oneline | head -2 && git diff HEAD~1 | grep '^[+-]' | cut -c1-1200"]], "output": "```console\n2ab10e1 Outline rev 5: one headline arm per tier\n2bd6790 Outline rev 4: break-even factor K/(K-1) per Brief rev 7\n--- a/Outline.md\n+++ b/Outline.md\n-# Outline (story-repo, revision 4 (C3 factor K/(K−1), Brief rev. 7); venue budget applied; answers idea-review #3 in story-repo)\n+# Outline (story-repo, revision 5 (headline arm per tier; C3 factor K/(K−1), Brief rev. 7); venue budget applied; answers idea-review #3 in story-repo)\n-T2 (f and X; census sets, alphafold indicators): B1 vector PPI++ (g = a + b′(f,X)) and OLS-adjusted mean (strongest linear baseline); B2 GBM-AIPW; B3 calibrated + X; B4 floor with X.\n+T2 (f and X; census sets, alphafold indicators): B1 vector PPI++ (g = a + b′(f,X)) and OLS-adjusted mean (strongest linear baseline); B2 GBM-AIPW; B3 calibrated + X; B4 floor with X; B5 the T2 **selection procedure** (choice among B1–B3 on folds separate from variance estimation, inside the loop).\n-**Headline measure and margin (set now; analysis may not move it after seeing results).** Paired width ratio r = width(arm)/width(A2 PPI++) on the same draws, with 95% CI over repetitions. Per cell: **no gain** if the CI of r lies inside [0.98, 1.02]; **gain** if the CI upper bound < 0.95 and the arm is valid (win rule of the Brief); **small / undecided** otherwise. Because width ∝ n^{-1/2}, r also reads as an effective label saving 1 − r² (r=0.95 ≈ 10% fewer labels); the paper states both. For each dataset at its reference n it reports: r of the best valid arm (A5/A6, and B-arms for T2), and r at the estimated floor (A7), both with CIs. The abstract's answer to \"how much shorter\" is the range of these numbers across sets, with the sets where it is no-gain named.\n+**Headline measure and margin (set now; analysis may not move it after seeing results).** Paired width ratio r = width(arm)/width(A2 PPI++) on the same draws, with 95% CI over repetitions. Per cell: **no gain** if the CI of r lies inside [0.98, 1.02]; **gain** if the CI upper bound < 0.95 and the arm is valid (win rule of the Brief); **small / undecided** otherwise. Because width ∝ n^{-1/2}, r also reads as an effective label saving 1 − r² (r=0.95 ≈ 10% fewer labels); the paper states both. **Headline arm, fixed before the runs: A6 for T1, B5 for T2.** C2 classifies each set on the headline arm's r with the margin above; no other arm decides the classification. For each dataset at its reference n the paper reports r of the headline arm and r at the estimated floor (A7), both with CIs; the best-of-arms r (a maximum over noisy ratios, biased toward gain) is shown only as a descriptive ceiling beside them. The abstract's answer to \"how much shorter\" is the range of these numbers across sets, with the sets where it is no-gain named.\n-| C2 | **Headroom on real data.** At each set's reference n, the best valid arm's paired width ratio vs PPI++ and the ratio at the estimated floor, classified by the margin above. (Refuted/qualified: ratios in [0.98, 1.02] on most sets means \"PPI++ is near the floor\"; that is a finding.) | E3 on 9 sets; ladder A0→A1→A2→A4/A5→B-arms→A7, SE factor and construction factor separated | Fig 2 (ladder: width ratio to PPI++ per set, CIs, validity flagged) + Table 1 (real n only: width, coverage with Wilson CI, r) ; full grid in appendix |\n+| C2 | **Headroom on real data.** At each set's reference n, the headline arm's (A6 / B5) paired width ratio vs PPI++ and the ratio at the estimated floor, classified by the margin above. (Refuted/qualified: ratios in [0.98, 1.02] on most sets means \"PPI++ is near the floor\"; that is a finding.) | E3 on 9 sets; ladder A0→A1→A2→A4/A5→B-arms→A7, SE factor and construction factor separated | Fig 2 (ladder: width ratio to PPI++ per set, CIs, validity flagged) + Table 1 (real n only: width, coverage with Wilson CI, r) ; full grid in appendix |\n-| C1 | **Floor + exact gap (setup).** Var of the pooled estimator = Var(Y)/M + (1/n−1/M)Var(Y−g); PPI++ equals the pooled linear estimator; excess over the floor = (1/n\n[truncated for publication]", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "11a91a7b-18e6-5dcf-8309-91fcb6f1e141", "at": 1795677850.9888854, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 16298, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.session/request_permission", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "e604117e04531bf705fecebc53613d19", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "message", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "session/request_permission"}, {"id": "f6e11a2d-5ceb-5927-9931-739cc0f5b4c9", "at": 1795677850.9924726, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 16299, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.response", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "e604117e04531bf705fecebc53613d19", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "message", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "response"}, {"id": "22f87030-3fa5-5d4d-82eb-4e8d3bf19591", "at": 1795677850.9945948, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 16300, "layer": "worker", "category": "usage", "kind": "session.usage", "role": "idea-review-repo", "actor": "idea-review-repo", "execution_id": "e604117e04531bf705fecebc53613d19", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "usage", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "context usage"}, {"id": "952c67d6-cece-54b6-8530-ac49db045ba5", "at": 1795677851.0496306, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 16301, "layer": "worker", "category": "chal